{
  "id": "association_replay_and_truncation",
  "title": "A repeated storage transaction creates no phantom Resource identity and no exactly-once claim, while discarded history is reported as explicit truncation and survives replay",
  "obligations": [
    "fixed-case-8",
    "invariant-3",
    "invariant-6",
    "route-metadata-http",
    "route-promql-info"
  ],
  "routes": [
    "columnar"
  ],
  "retained_version_limit": 2,
  "open_decisions": [
    "3",
    "6",
    "7",
    "9"
  ],
  "scope": "Settled retry and truncation semantics only. The fixture repeats an ordinary storage transaction with the same sample and metadata: the duplicate sample rejects the whole retry, so it is neither an exactly-once delivery claim nor a phantom Resource identity. The evidence is at the storage commit boundary; no OTAP/OTLP reset or receiver-retry semantics are claimed, and transport acknowledgements do not imply exactly-once delivery. A metadata-only repetition of the same content and effective time is idempotent and an observation at another point only extends bounds. The retained_version_limit of 2 is the available bounded-history mechanism; once it discards the oldest version the fixture requires explicit history_truncated reporting, absence rather than a resurrected answer, and the same truncated state after WAL replay. It does not decide an explicit clearing or expiry operation (decision 3), an admission or coalescing budget (decision 9), PR99 grammar (decision 6) or a snapshot cursor (decision 7).",
